 \subsection{Logical consistency}
 
-We verified the logical consistency of the knowledge graph using the
-ELK reasoner, as described in Section~\ref{methods}. We loaded the
-tractable set of OWL modules into a single OWL ontology and ran ELK to
-check for unsatisfiable classes and contradictory axioms. The reasoner
-reported the merged included modules as consistent.
-
-We performed this consistency check as part of continuous integration
-during ontology development. Whenever we modified the TBox or
-introduced new ABox modules, we re-ran the ELK reasoner to detect
-regressions. We chose ELK over more expressive reasoners such as
-HermiT \cite{hermit} because the size of the knowledge graph
-made reasoning in the full OWL 2 DL profile computationally
-infeasible.
-
-We implemented the ELK consistency check via the OWL API (v4.5.26) with the ELK OWL API module (v0.4.3) in a Groovy
-script. A clean rerun on the current tractable modules loaded 781,293
-axioms from 10 distinct ontology IRIs and reported the resulting
-merged ontology as consistent; the generated ecosystem and
-laboratory-control modules were included. The run excluded the
-multi-gigabyte taxonomy ABox and large reference modules and did not
-precompute the class hierarchy, so it establishes bounded consistency
-of the included modules rather than whole-graph OWL~2~DL consistency.
-ELK is incomplete for universal restrictions, inverse properties and
-other constructs used by imported modules, and the excluded ABoxes
-were not covered; likewise, the selected forward-rule program of
-Section~\ref{subsec:owl_rdf} has not been demonstrated to reach a
-fixed point and is not complete OWL-Horst closure \cite{owlhorst}.
